Power-on-reset circuit for SG13CMOS5L for a 1.2V power supply
--------------------------------------------------------------
Specifications:
Operating voltage:  1.2V (up to 1.5V)
Reset delay time:   40ms

Inputs:
	"ena" is a circuit enable and normally should be tied to
		the 1.2V power supply unless in a test environment.

Outputs:
	"por_unbuf" is for testing and should not be used
	"porb" is low during the initial power-up and high after the
		delay period.
	"por" is high during the initial power-up and low after the
		delay period.

Normally "porb" is used to drive an internal chip digital reset because
it has clean low and high states, whereas "por" follows the supply voltage
ramp at initial power-up.

History:  The Caravel harness chip design that Efabless made for the Open
MPW shuttle run featured a PoR circuit with a simple timer based on a trickle
current onto a capacitor to set an approximatly 15ms delay between the 3.3V
power supply being turned on and the power-on-reset signal being forced low.

This specific version is self-calibrated which makes it more sensitive to the
power supply level;  it can be improved by replacing the resistor/transistor
stack at the "inn" input of the amplifier with a bandgap, since the circuit
is designed for a value of 1.2V.

Power supplies with an extremely long rise time (> 0.1s) will cause the reset
to trip at a low voltage (about 0.9V), which should still be sufficient for a
proper reset.

In a dual-voltage environment, this circuit should always be connected to 1.2V.
